// tap-echo: --buffer 65535 --close-after 0
// Parsed SYN gets a challenge ACK before ACK/window validation (RFC 5961 4.2).
// Socket::process ignores the SYN's contents and preserves the connection state;
// challenge_ack_allowed shares the per-socket 500 ms limit. The packets after the
// challenge ACK verify that the connection still closes normally.
// Ensure that the reception of a TCP SYN with SEG.SEQ = RCV.NXT + RCV.WND
// in the FIN-WAIT-1 state triggers the sending of a challenge ACK.
// Adapted from freebsd-net/tcp-testsuite commit 04b8c73fc5081fe3792f1bed9168c9b22aaedddb,
// state-event-engine/rcv-syn-fin-wait-1/rcv-syn-fin-wait-1-outside-right-secure-ipv4.pkt
// (BSD-2-Clause; notice below). Changes for Zhida's packet-only runner, tap-echo
// being the application (fuzz/packetdrill/README.md, "Upstream suites"):
// - IPv4 variant. Dropped: sysctls, which set FreeBSD defaults and
//   net.inet.tcp.insecure_syn=0 (RFC 5961 SYN checks on); Zhida has no such switch;
//   fcntl; getsockopt(SO_RCVBUF); --tolerance_usecs=75000 (the runner keeps
//   packetdrill's 4 ms); sockstat and tcpdrop commands except where replaced below.
// - Upstream reaches the state by an active open (connect; FreeBSD's SYN, the
//   peer's SYN-ACK <mss 1460,sackOK>, FreeBSD's ACK). tap-echo only listens, so the
//   client opens instead: the client's SYN carries the options upstream's peer sent
//   (same negotiated result: MSS 1460, SACK, no window scale, no timestamps), as in
//   upstream's own passive-open scripts (rcv-rst-syn-rcvd via-listen), then Zhida's
//   SYN-ACK and the client's ACK 0.1 s later.
// - shutdown(SHUT_WR) becomes tap-echo --close-after 0: Zhida sends its FIN right
//   after the handshake (upstream: 0.1 s later).
// - Receive window: tap-echo --buffer 65535, so Zhida's RCV.WND is 65535, its
//   advertised window (no window scale). Upstream's RCV.WND is 65700 (FreeBSD's 65536-byte
//   buffer rounded up to whole MSS), so its edge sequence number 65701 becomes 65536.
// - getsockopt(SO_ERROR) = 0 dropped; the packets after it show the connection alive.
//
